e screen or look  at the description to check out my  disclaimer including a trigger warning  to those with current or previous  experiences with disordered eating so as  always feel free to skip this video if  it's not supportive to your journey and  if you are not already subscribed hit  that subscribe button and don't forget  to follow me over on tick tock and  instagram abby's kitchen also a huge  thank you to my colleague eric  williamson who gave me tons and tons of  amazing important insight into this  topic so i am going to be leaving his  information down below all right let's  kick things off here first things first  what exactly is lumen here we go again  well a lot of you showed up in my dms to  ask about this new technology after  blogilati's partnered with them on her  instagram page in march so for my  birthday sam bought me this thing  because he knows i love devices that  tell me more about my body it's called a  lumen and he told me it might help me  improve my metabolism so every morning i  blow into it and it tells me if i'm  burning fat or carbs it's measuring my  metabolic flexibility which is my body's  ability to switch between burning fat or  carbs for feel it'll score you between  one to five and based on what score you  get lumen will recommend some  macronutrients to help you burn more fat  so right now i'm going super high  protein i'm also trying to sleep eight  hours a day because if i do not my body  will be in stress mode and basically  it'll be harder for me to burn fat now  all of this so that i can build more  muscle next month i'll be focused on fat  burn because it'll be time to put on  that sparkly bikini and prepare for that  faux bikini competition stage walk now  you've probably seen other endorsements  from people like steve farina lizabeth  lopez health coach kate dr caroline leaf  heidi powell and so many more and i also  want to share that i have also been  approached for a partnership multiple  times and have respectfully declined  everyone  how about new including one time when  they cryptically let us on about a new  wellness technology only to tell us by  like the 10th email that surprise it's  actually lubin not cool pr peeps not  cool but bottom line my team and i have  done a lot of research on this tool in  short lumen is a handheld  breathalyzer-like device that claims to  hack your metabolism by making it more  efficient improving metabolic  flexibility enhancing fat burning and  promoting weight loss what's in your  life the technology works by analyzing  your breath to determine if your body is  burning fat or it's burning carbs and  based on this information it will then  recommend a meal plan to keep you in the  fat burning zone for weight loss but can  lumen really walk the talk or talk the  walk or whatever that phrase is and more  importantly does this even help you lose  weight well first of all let's talk  about the legitimacy or lack thereof  with this technology so basically when  you breathe into the lumen device it  claims to calculate what we call the  respiratory exchange ratio aka rer which  looks at your co2 to oxygen levels rer  is an indicator of our source of fuel  which then gives lumen data about our  metabolic flexibility level i'm going to  speak more to that in just a moment but  basically if your result reads a high  co2 to oxygen ratio you're predominantly  burning carbs but if you have a low co2  reading it means that your body is using  fat now when looking at the data on the  device eric agreed that this device had  pretty good within participant  reliability but a weak relationship with  measured rer so in other words it may be  able to tell you how your metabolic  flexibility is changing over time but  not if you are more flexible than let's  say your friend  yes which is valuable but not everything  it's also important to point out that  most of the validation studies that  lumens cites are conducted by lumen  themselves which have not been peer  reviewed and are subject to bias there  was one external study conducted by san  francisco state university which  concluded that lumen is comparable to  the gold standard for measuring rer and  might be an effective way to monitor  changes in metabolic responses however  it's important to note that two of the  study's authors also work for metaflo  which is the company that founded lumen  which some may consider a source of  external bias obviously this doesn't  necessarily mean that lumen doesn't  accurately measure metabolism i actually  think that it probably does but the  question remains whether or not it will  help you hack said metabolism as it  claims so let's talk about the most  important piece of this puzzle metabolic  flexibility so i briefly discussed  metabolic flexibility in my video on  intuitive fasting but in short it refers  to your body's ability to switch between  carbs and fat for fuel definition wise  it's the change in fuel oxidation to  fuel availability which is measured as  the change in rer but according to the  folks at lumen the more flexible your  metabolism the more efficiently you can  lose weight and their advice allegedly  helps give you that edge well lumen  aside there are a few evidence-based  ways to improve our metabolic  flexibility largely related to either  mitochondrial biogenesis or insulin  sensitivity so those are exercise and  fitness leanness and body composition  and a negative energy balance the  mitochondria piece is pretty complicated  but it is important in the context of  athletes and sports nutrition but for  more lay people insulin sensitivity is  directly related to our metabolic  flexibility because the more sensitive  our cells are to insulin the more  effectively they are able to use up  carbs for energy then once those carbs  are all used up the body is then able to  tap into fat stores for energy but  insulin resistance makes it more  difficult for your cells to respond to  insulin and use up carbs for energy and  therefore it can't so easily switch to  burning fat this is such a complicated  topic but the relationship between  insulin resistance and metabolic  inflexibility may explain why impaired  metabolic flexibility is linked to  things like obesity diabetes and  metabolic syndrome but aside from  physical fitness body composition and  energy balance can we hack our  metabolism into being more flexible just  by following a specific diet well this  is essentially what lumen suggests when  you breathe into the device you get a  score from one to five which determines  if you're burning predominantly carbs  fat or both lumen will then recommend a  daily meal plan that's either low medium  or high carb lumen doesn't explicitly  recommend restrictive diets and i would  say that the meal plans are pretty  customizable and user friendly which we  love good for us the focus here seems to  be primarily on adjusting the relative  macronutrient ranges depending on where  you score and while i haven't used the  device personally myself i have read a  lot of people's reviews and i do have  some concerns with the approach for one  lumen recommends fasting overnight which  does make sense in this case because we  know that when we starve the body of  fuel overnight we dry up our glycogen  stores which then allows us to start to  burn into our fat stores so in the  morning we will generally register as  burning fat which is obviously what most  lay people are going to want to see most  of the time and this is a totally normal  healthy metabolic pattern assuming that  you're not in a caloric surplus i've  also read that most people find that  they're given instructions to eat low  carb for like several days in a row  before finally getting a moderate or  high carb meal and i can see why they do  this too it's probably very encouraging  to see that you are often burning fat  which is what would happen if you were  consistently eating low carb this is  also what happens all the time if you're  doing straight up keto but if you were  doing straight up keto your body would  adapt to fat oxidation and may actually  acutely make you more metabolically  inflexible and unable to efficiently use  carbs when you cheat and have a side of  fries you actually want to see a high  rer when you eat carbs because it's the  change in rer not a chronically low rer  that denotes a strong metabolic  flexibility so i appreciate that lumen  tries to address the shortcomings of  like a continuous low carb or keto diet  by giving you an occasional higher carb  day to keep your metabolism kind of like  nimble but i also worry that most people  will not appreciate the nuance of a  score system like this and the actual  long-term goal the actual goal should  not to always see a low lumen number  like a one or a two which indicates that  you're burning fat if that's what you  want just do keto and like never ever  eat a piece of bread again but that  sucks  the gold lumen should be to see  variation in those numbers depending on  what you ate you want to burn carbs when  you eat carbs and you want to burn fat  when you eat fat i'm going to speak more  to the dangerous psychological  assumptions that this may promote in a  moment but i do think it may be missing  the forest from the trees and that  forest is the goal of long-term  metabolic flexibility it may be exciting  to see that you're burning fat but  unless your diet choices are directly  changing your body fat insulin  sensitivity fitness level or body  composition its effects on long-term  metabolism hacking will likely  underwhelm but let's just say you were  able to improve your metabolic  flexibility with a device like lumen  would that necessarily help you lose  weight well probably not all on its own  but as eric pointed out maybe indirectly  so like i said the primary factors  driving metabolic flexibility are  exercise and leanness so metabolic  flexibility is kind of more like the  outcome rather than the solution per se  for losing weight it may help but it's  not the end-all be-all so a program like  lumen may support weight loss if someone  sees that they're in the fat burning  zone after like a workout on the app or  like after a really low carb meal and  they feel motivated to continue in this  calorie deficit way of life but at the  end of the day it's you  not the device that has to do the work  only you can prevent wildfire so whether  you're burning carbs or fat for fuel  eating a low medium or a high carb diet  you've still got to put yourself in a  calorie deficit or exercise to lose that  weight you're not going to burn fat if  you're in a calorie surplus even if the  app says that you are and carbohydrate  metabolism may be one small piece of the  puzzle but it's likely overshadowed by  other factors like total calories  consumed exercise sleep stress and  genetics you also have to consider the  potential downsides to a gadget like  this so lumen makes it seem like you can  magically fix your metabolism just by  blowing into a device something fishy  going on here but what seems to get  downplayed in a lot of the influencer  ads is the fact that you have to  literally plan your whole day of eating  in the morning around whatever the  stupid app says you can't meal prep you  can't make plans and if you get told  it's a low-carb day you have to cancel  your pasta date night your work drinks  or your birthday cake with friends  i also think it risks us immediately  assigning a lot of guilt and fear around  certain foods based on a random reading  for example if you had pizza for dinner  and the next morning saw that you're  still burning all those carbs you might  feel really bad that you've been kicked  out of the fat burning mode and start to  see carbs in a really negative light  i've read a lot of reviews of people  waking up every morning and feeling  completely defeated and in an automatic  bad mood when they score a high four or  five after a night with friends and like  i said this is actually totally normal  so waiting to eat the next day until you  register your burning fat again feels  incredibly disorderly it's just as  obsessive and intrusive as weighing  yourself multiple times a day for  external data so it definitely can be a  very slippery slope at the end of the  day if weight loss is the goal we know  how important diet tolerance and  adherence is hence why the research  tells us that weight loss outcomes on  low carb and low fat diets are pretty  much the same it comes down to what  works for you based on what foods you  enjoy if you really like oatmeal for  breakfast you're going to be in a  sourpuss mood if you have to eat eggs  and avocado so please  somebody tell me how is any minute and  transient improvements in metabolic  flexibility going to be worth letting a  tiny expensive handheld device run your  social life and your relationship with  food hard pass from me so in conclusion  well i do think that lumen might provide  some interesting data on your metabolism  and the factors that may impact it like  diet sleep and exercise and i'm so  excited to see the technology evolve any  weight loss outcomes are really going to  depend on your ability to stick to a  diet that creates a caloric deficit your  body is just too damn smart to have it  be hacked by a simple app you are still  responsible for said hack and on that  note that is all that i have for you  guys today if you like this video be  sure to give it the thumbs up leave me a  comment below on what you'd like to see  me review next subscribe to the channel  and i'll see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  [Music]

